.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd November 6, 2026
.Dt AFTERBOOT 8
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm afterboot
.Nd building, booting, and checking the operator image
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This page is for the operator of an ABSD system: how to get the operator
image, boot it, check it, and look after it, and what it cannot do yet.
It describes the served
.Ql net fsd
operator composition, whose file server holds the disk and whose session
service provides SSH and two enrolled local text sessions.
Local login and init's read-only kernel maintenance do not require networking;
see
.Xr local-console 7 .
Exact-image qualification and hardware limitations are separate from the
facilities described here (see
.Xr intro 1 ,
.Sx QUALIFICATION ) .
.Ss Downloaded preview
If you started a downloaded preview with
.Nm absd-try ,
you have already booted the machine and enrolled your SSH key.
The
.Pa QUICKSTART.md
in your downloaded release files on the host explains how to start it again.
.Pp
To stop cleanly, run
.Ic shutdown
in this session and wait for the machine to halt.
Ctrl-C in the host's
.Nm absd-try
also requests a clean shutdown.
If it cannot confirm shutdown, it leaves the machine running and prints
instructions for trying again.
A second Ctrl-C while it is waiting forces a stop; the next boot may need recovery.
.Pp
After a clean halt, start the same machine directory again with
.Nm absd-try .
Files saved in your home directory remain for the next boot.
Do not create a replacement machine just to restart it.
.Ss Build the image
The operator image is built on a Linux host from the ABSD platform
repository and a checkout of the ABSD kernel at the commit the platform's
.Pa scripts/check-all.sh
names:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f operator-key
$ ABSD_KERNEL_RUN=/path/to/kernel-checkout \e
    scripts/build-operator-image.sh \e
    --authorized-keys operator-key.pub [--etc DIR] [--disk-profile 128m|2g] OUT
.Ed
.Pp
It builds the platform, then writes
.Pa OUT/absd-operator.iso
(GRUB, the kernel, init, the file server, and the SSH server),
.Pa OUT/absd-operator.img
(a 128 MiB disk by default), and
.Pa OUT/MANIFEST
(the two commits and the SHA-256 of every file installed).
For a larger fresh disk, select
.Ql --disk-profile 2g :
2 GiB of device space, with a 1 GiB filesystem region.
The default
.Ql 128m
profile has a 64 MiB filesystem region.
Filesystem metadata and installed files use part of each region.
The profile applies only to fresh construction; it does not grow an existing disk.
Host inspection and recovery read the whole disk into memory, and backup or
restore may allocate a full copy even when the input file is sparse.
The disk holds every command under
.Pa /bin ,
this manual under
.Pa /share/man ,
.Pa /etc/network.conf
and
.Pa /etc/sshd.conf
(the files in
.Ar DIR
in place of the defaults), and the keys under
.Pa /ssh/authorized_keys ,
which must be
.Ql ssh-ed25519
lines without options.
Files are written by ABSD itself, in importer boots under QEMU, and the host
reads every one back before the build succeeds.
The first boot adds
.Pa /home/operator ,
.Pa /tmp ,
.Pa /var/log ,
and the host key.
.Ss Boot
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ scripts/boot-operator.sh OUT 2222
.Ed
.Pp
This runs QEMU in the foreground with RDRAND
.Pq Ql -cpu max,vendor=GenuineIntel,+rdrand ,
the ISO, the disk as the primary IDE master
.Pq Xr ata 4 ,
one e1000 card on QEMU's user network with 127.0.0.1:2222 forwarded to
the address and port the disk's
.Pa /etc
files name, and the serial console in
.Pa OUT/console.log .
It returns when the machine halts
.Pq status 0
or the boot fails (QEMU's status, 7 for a failed boot).
A
.Xr reboot 8
boots the same image again within the same run.
Without RDRAND the SSH server refuses to start
.Pq Ql sshd: entropy: ... .
When FSD and local enrollment remain usable, SSH-specific startup failures
can leave the local-only service available; otherwise see the maintenance
and recovery policy below.
The script holds
.Pa OUT/recovery/lock
for its run, keeps the previous console as
.Pa OUT/console-<time>.log ,
and runs the recovery precheck before QEMU starts
.Pq status 7 when it refuses .
.Ss Recover after an unclean stop
A machine that stopped without
.Xr shutdown 8
or
.Xr reboot 8
(power loss, a killed QEMU, a fault after readiness) is refused on its
next boot:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
RECOVERY WRITABLE REFUSED reason=storage_fence previous=failed fence=none
.Ed
.Pp
with status 7 and nothing written.
The only way back to writable service on the same image is the explicit
operator act, on the host, with the machine stopped:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ python3 -I scripts/release.py backup --target OUT BACKUP
$ python3 -I scripts/release.py recover --target OUT --status
$ python3 -I scripts/release.py recover --target OUT \e
    --attest-sole-instance --accept-committed-state --reason "why"
$ scripts/boot-operator.sh OUT 2222            # another terminal
$ python3 -I scripts/release.py recover --target OUT --wait-ready --id ID
.Ed
.Pp
The act records what it saw (the committed state, its file identity and
content hash, the reason) under
.Pa OUT/recovery/
and writes one record on the disk; the next served boot admits exactly that
state, consumes the record as its first write, and reports
.Ql RECOVERY WRITABLE RESUMED id=... .
Synced files are exact; a file that was being overwritten may hold a mix
of old and new sectors; unsynced growth is lost.
.Ql recover --revoke
withdraws a pending authorization; every other host tool refuses
.Ql recovery_pending
while one is on the disk.
The procedure, every refusal and its action, and the limits are in the
operator runbook, section 9a, and in
.Pa docs/WRITABLE-ENTRY.md .
.Ss Check the host key
The SSH server prints its host key's fingerprint on the console at every
start, in plain text, in the form
.Ql ssh-keygen -l
uses, and the public key itself:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ grep '^sshd: host key' OUT/console.log
sshd: host key 256 SHA256:3n0X...Q (ED25519)
sshd: host key ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nz...
.Ed
.Pp
Compare the
.Ql SHA256:
value with the one
.Nm ssh
shows on the first connection
.Pq Dq ED25519 key fingerprint is SHA256:...
before accepting it, or put the public key in a known_hosts file yourself
and keep strict checking on:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ echo "[127.0.0.1]:2222 ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nz..." > known_hosts
$ ssh -p 2222 -i operator-key -o UserKnownHostsFile=known_hosts \e
    -o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes operator@127.0.0.1
absd$
.Ed
.Pp
The same lines are in
.Pa /var/log/sshd .
The file server creates the key at the first boot
.Pq Ql FSD HOST KEY GENERATED
and loads it after
.Pq Ql FSD HOST KEY PRESENT .
It lives at
.Pa /ssh/host_ed25519_seed
on the disk; no session can read, list, or reach
.Pa /ssh ,
but anyone holding the disk image can read the key.
.Pp
Only public keys are accepted.
A line of
.Pa /ssh/authorized_keys
with options or another key type makes the server refuse the whole file.
There are no passwords and no accounts; the user name is logged and
otherwise ignored.
Every key in the file is the same single operator.
.Ss Look around
.Bd -literal -offset indent
absd$ uname -a
absd$ release
absd$ date
absd$ uptime
absd$ ls /
absd$ df
absd$ man intro
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql ls /
shows the session's view:
.Pa bin/ ,
.Pa etc/ ,
.Pa home/ ,
.Pa share/ ,
.Pa tmp/ ,
.Pa var/ .
.Nm df
prints the filesystem's size, the blocks in use and free, the commit
sequence on the disk, and this session's mounts with their access; every
session shares one filesystem and there are no quotas.
.Nm release
names the release being served: the commands in
.Pa /bin
and the manual in
.Pa /share
belong to it, and everything else to this machine
.Pq Xr release 1 .
.Ss Where things live
.Bl -tag -width "/tmp/session-N"
.It Pa /home/operator
Read-write.
Kept across sessions and boots; shared by every session.
Put your files here.
.It Pa /tmp/session-N
Read-write, this session only: no other session can see it.
It is removed when the session ends.
.It Pa /bin
The commands.
Read-only.
.It Pa /etc
The configuration,
.Xr network.conf 5
and
.Xr sshd.conf 5 .
Read-only.
.It Pa /share
This manual
.Pq Pa /share/man .
Read-only.
.It Pa /var/log
The bounded boot/service history and retained owner network sample; see
.Xr logs 1
and
.Xr net-status 1 .
Read-only.
.It Pa /ssh
The host key and authorized keys.
Not visible to any session.
.El
.Pp
Read-only means read-only for sessions: those files are placed when the
image is built or reconfigured, and no session changes them.
.Ss Configuration
.Pa /etc/network.conf
sets the address, netmask, and gateway;
.Pa /etc/sshd.conf
sets the port, the number of connections, the login grace time, the idle
timeout, and the authentication tries.
The SSH server reads both at start and prints what it uses:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sshd: network 10.0.2.15/24 gateway 10.0.2.2 (/etc/network.conf)
sshd: port 22, at most 4 connections, login grace 30 s, idle
timeout 1800 s, 6 authentication tries (/etc/sshd.conf)
.Ed
.Pp
A missing file means the defaults, and the line says so.
A malformed file is refused: the console says which file, which line, and
why, the SSH server does not start, and the boot fails.
Sessions cannot write
.Pa /etc ,
so a session can never lock the operator out.
To change or fix a file, halt the machine and replace the file on the
image, then boot again:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ABSD_KERNEL_RUN=/path/to/kernel-checkout \e
    scripts/build-operator-image.sh --reconfigure --etc DIR OUT
.Ed
.Pp
Each file of
.Ar DIR
replaces its namesake whole, in one commit; everything else on the disk
is kept.
.Fl -authorized-keys Ar KEYS
replaces the keys the same way.
.Pp
This is for an
.Ar OUT
made by
.Ql build-operator-image.sh ;
its
.Pa MANIFEST
then names the new digests.
Before the image is touched, the command refuses an
.Ar OUT
without a
.Pa MANIFEST ,
a malformed one, or a left-over
.Pa MANIFEST.new .
It also refuses a replacement path that is a directory, files the
filesystem has no room for, and a kernel build that fails.
A machine installed with
.Ql scripts/release.py install
(its
.Ar OUT
holds
.Pa TARGET
or
.Pa RELEASES )
is refused the same way.
Change its configuration with
.Ql scripts/release.py reconfigure ,
which writes with the selected release's own importer and records a
receipt and a ledger line (docs/OPERATOR-RUNBOOK.md, section 13).
Earlier versions of this command imported the files into such a machine
and then failed; it also used to mark the guard dirty before these checks.
.Pp
If the importer boot fails, the disk's guard stays dirty, and the files
before the failing one may already be replaced; every later write and
boot refuses until the disk is recovered offline.
If the image took the files but
.Pa MANIFEST
could not be written, the command says so; run it again once the fault is
fixed.
.Ss Logs
.Xr dmesg 1
reads the current boot's bounded volatile kernel ring.
.Xr logs 1
reads retained boot and service records, follows new records and exports
an escaped record stream.
.Pp
Legacy text lines use UTC software civil collection time; typed retained
records keep their original boot/tick identity. Clock events explain civil
corrections, and no current anchor is applied retroactively to old records:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
absd$ logs --list-boots
absd$ logs -u sshd -n 20
absd$ logs -u fsd -f
absd$ logs -b -1 --export > /home/operator/previous-boot.txt
.Ed
.Pp
A dedicated collector persists kernel and managed-service stdout, stderr
and lifecycle records with boot, source and sequence attribution.
Init captures service output into the kernel ring independently of FSD.
The journal retains the current and one previous boot within fixed bounds;
loss, truncation, suppression and persistence failures are explicit.
Use
.Ql logs --health
for collector state, and
.Ql logs --volatile
for the independent ring view when persistent history is unavailable.
Follow remains foreground work; Ctrl-C ends it.
.Pp
The existing
.Pa /var/log/fsd
and
.Pa /var/log/sshd
text projections remain available for commands such as
.Ql tail -n 3 /var/log/sshd .
There is no
.Ql tail -f ;
use
.Ql logs -f
for the journal instead.
A retained record is not proof that its condition is still active.
Successful flushing is required for persistence across reboot; final fault
output, complete history and abrupt power-loss durability are not guaranteed.
See
.Xr logs 1
for exact retention, authority, gap and shutdown semantics.
.Ss Copying files
OpenSSH's
.Nm sftp
and default modern
.Nm scp
use
.Xr absd-sftp 8 .
Use the same client-visible SSH endpoint, key, and host key pinning.
Relative SFTP paths start in
.Pa /home/operator .
Metadata preservation and legacy
.Nm scp
with
.Fl O
are unsupported; the subsystem manual names its other bounds.
The command-and-redirection transport also remains available from the client:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ssh -p 2222 operator@127.0.0.1 'cat > /home/operator/f' < f
$ ssh -p 2222 operator@127.0.0.1 'cat >> /home/operator/f' < more
$ ssh -p 2222 operator@127.0.0.1 cat /home/operator/f > f
.Ed
.Pp
Under QEMU (TCG) a 1 MiB file took 45 to 60 s up and about 17 s down.
Both directions are byte-exact for any file, binary included, as long as
no terminal is requested (no
.Fl t ;
a terminal changes line endings and takes control characters as keys).
A command with a
.Ql | ,
.Ql < ,
.Ql > ,
.Ql \&; ,
.Ql & ,
or
.Ql \&"
is run by
.Xr absd-sh 1
.Po
.Ql absd-sh -c
.Pc ,
and so is a command whose program is a path other than
.Pa /bin/ Ns Ar name
(an uploaded program:
.Ql ssh host /home/operator/prog in.txt ) ;
any other command runs directly.
Paths are absolute, or relative to
.Pa / ,
which only reads.
The upload is committed when
.Nm cat
ends; check it with
.Ql ssh host sha256sum FILE .
An upload the client breaks off leaves the file cut short (the old
contents are gone once
.Ql >
opens it).
Nothing of the upload keeps running after the client goes: the shell ends
.Nm cat
and exits.
Nothing is copied that the session could not write itself.
.Ss What is persistent
A file written under
.Pa /home/operator
is committed to the disk when it is closed, when
.Nm sync
is run, when the session ends, and at most about 200 ms after a write that
was not otherwise committed.
.Nm sync
returns once everything the file server has accepted, from every
session, is on the disk.
After a commit the file survives the machine stopping abruptly; see
.Xr filesystem 7 .
.Ss Time
.Xr date 1
reads the boot-local software civil clock in UTC, to whole seconds.
Use date --status to inspect its source, age, staleness and last failure.
The initial RTC source is unverified. Enrolled/authenticated shells may use
clock set UNIX_SECONDS to assert civil time, and clock sync to request a
bounded configured unauthenticated network estimate; see
.Xr clock 1
and
.Xr time.conf 5 .
Corrections do not write the RTC, survive reboot, alter monotonic deadlines,
or establish TLS validation trust.
.Xr uptime 1
counts kernel ticks since boot and names the boot.
A file records one time, its modification time
.Pq Xr filesystem 7 ;
stat shows it as UTC ISO time (or unknown), and ls -l shows UTC to the
minute (or -). No command sets it.
.Ss Limits
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Four accepted SSH connections at once, the default and the most
.Xr sshd.conf 5
admits, counting unauthenticated ones. Local-console reservations are separate.
A further completed TCP connection is promptly closed without an SSH
session slot, file-server binding, or banner. The console records
.Ql SSH GLOBAL REFUSED ... limit=4 .
Once a session releases its slot, an authenticated connection can use it.
.It
At most two accepted unauthenticated connections from one IPv4 source
while global slots remain. A further one is closed with
.Ql SSH PREAUTH REFUSED ... reason=source-limit .
An authenticated connection still counts toward the global four.
This source limit applies after TCP accept, not to the SYN/listener backlog.
.It
Thirty-two process slots in the current served profile, including init, the
file server, the session service and the log collector when enabled.
.It
128 handles per process, 32 live cells and 128 live pipes in the
current served profile. These are separate limits: one process can reach
its handle bound before exhausting the global pipe table.
Printed limits and occupancy counts alone do not show that every table
was exhausted in a served boot.
.It
Commands cannot start programs; only a session's shell can
.Pq Xr absd-sh 1 .
When a session ends, its shell ends the commands it started.
If the SSH server has to kill the shell instead
.Pq Ql SSH SESSION END ... forced=true ,
those commands can outlive the session. A
.Xr shutdown 8
that finds them after its wait bound stops with a named failure; a post-ready
.Xr reboot 8
can escalate to a whole-machine recovery reset instead of a clean restart.
Neither outcome proves process-tree reclamation or sync of unfinished writes.
.It
Twenty file-server channels in this boot: one worker control channel,
one private logger channel, and eighteen channels reserved for ordinary
sessions. Two local shells reserve six channels and four SSH shells reserve
twelve, with one primary channel and two command spares per shell.
These reservations do not add process slots or guarantee capacity for every
concurrent command. See
.Xr absd-sh 1 .
.It
One CPU by default, one selected network card and one disk.
The host's ABSD_OPERATOR_CPUS setting can select candidate two-, four-
or six-CPU operator profiles with a matching image and launcher.
They are separate from kernel-only AP fixtures; a result for one profile
does not qualify another. See the platform
.Pa docs/OPERATOR-RUNBOOK.md
for construction and exact-profile evidence requirements.
.El
.Ss How the boot ends
The boot runs until the operator asks for
.Xr shutdown 8
or
.Xr reboot 8 ,
from
.Ql ssh host shutdown
or from an interactive shell; every session is told and ends, and the file
server commits everything before the machine halts or restarts.
Stopping QEMU otherwise is an abrupt stop: anything not successfully
synced may be lost or partially written, and a dirty writable image may
refuse its next boot until offline recovery.
.Pp
Essential session-service or FSD failure triggers init's bounded cleanup.
After readiness, the advertised recovery budget may permit a visible
whole-machine reset of the same selected image. This does not replace a
service in place or guarantee that writable startup will succeed.
Before readiness, on unknown or exhausted budget, or when reset is refused,
a local-enabled image holds read-only kernel maintenance. An external
operator reboot is required to leave that held state; dirty-image recovery
remains a separate host-side act. The headless profile can stop instead.
The log collector alone has a bounded process-restart policy;
see
.Xr logs 1 .
.Ss What you cannot do yet
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Change the configuration from a session, or without a reboot.
.It
Run a remote syslog service or automatic log forwarding.
Use
.Xr logs 1
for foreground follow or explicit export instead.
.It
Control arbitrary processes of other sessions.
.Ql ps -A
provides read-only system-wide snapshots; the bare
.Xr ps 1
builtin remains session-scoped.
.It
Add users; there is one operator.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
On the console
.Pq Pa OUT/console.log :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql sshd: /etc/FILE: line N: REASON; fix the file ...; sshd is not running
SSH configuration was refused; a local-only service may remain available
when its enrollment and filesystem are usable.
Fix the halted image with
.Fl -reconfigure .
.It Ql FSD FAILED mount: ...
the file server refused the disk (a damaged or foreign filesystem).
The filesystem is unavailable to sessions; local-enabled init can retain
read-only kernel maintenance. There is no on-box repair tool; see
.Xr filesystem 7 .
.El
.Pp
Read service output with
.Xr logs 1
and kernel diagnostics with
.Xr dmesg 1 .
Init suppresses asynchronous physical-console replay in the local-enabled
served image so it does not overwrite the command being entered.
Some headless or qualification modes also emit init's hex-framed
.Ql EXTERNAL OUTPUT CHUNK ... data=HEX
records; do not treat their absence on a managed terminal as missing capture.
